# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
('H1', '1. Introduction'),
('H2', '1.1 Background and Problem Statement'),
('P', 'Indian capital markets have seen an unprecedented inflow of individual investors. By 31 January 2026 the National Stock Exchange reported 12.7 crore unique registered investors and more than 25 crore trading accounts [1]. Growth in participation, however, has not been matched by growth in outcomes. A SEBI study covering more than one crore individual traders in equity futures and options found that 93% of them made losses between FY22 and FY24, with aggregate losses above Rs 1.8 lakh crore and an average of about Rs 2 lakh per trader [2].'),
('P', 'The underlying difficulty is not the availability of information. Prices, financial statements and shareholding disclosures are published by NSE and BSE at no cost. The difficulty is interpretation. A retail investor must know, for example, that a price-to-earnings multiple of 45 is moderate for FMCG but extreme for metals, that heavy promoter pledging has preceded several high-profile collapses, and that a price below the 200-day moving average usually signals institutional selling. Professional research that embeds such norms is typically restricted to paying clients or institutional mandates.'),
('H2', '1.2 Motivation'),
('P', 'Large language models can read filings, retrieve current figures and explain them in plain language. Three properties nevertheless make them hazardous as financial advisers: they can fabricate figures, their outputs vary between runs even at temperature zero, and their implicit benchmarks are drawn mainly from US markets. DhanVeda is built on the premise that these weaknesses can be contained by wrapping a general-purpose model in three layers: an explicit India-calibrated scoring rubric, a hierarchy of providers, and deterministic algorithms where judgement is not needed. Each layer makes a claim that can be measured, which is the purpose of this report.'),
('H2', '1.3 Objectives'),
('N', ['To deliver an end-to-end platform with 14 AI features spanning equity research, decision hygiene, green finance, portfolio review and venture analysis.',
       'To keep the marginal cost of analysis close to zero by routing across free-tier providers without sacrificing availability.',
       'To ground every score in an explicit, India-specific rubric that can be audited rule by rule.',
       'To evaluate that design using the methods expected of empirical research: pre-registered hypotheses, controlled variables, repeated trials and uncertainty estimates.']),
('H2', '1.4 Contributions'),
('B', ['A five-tier, failure-aware LLM hierarchy that includes a two-stage collaborative mode in which one model retrieves live data and a second model scores it (Chapter 3).',
       'A token-budget engineering method that compresses a 3,645-token rubric to 1,571 tokens, verified with production-grade BPE tokenizers (Chapter 4).',
       'A transparent ESG algorithm for 532 listed securities built on SEBI pledge and BRSR signals, together with a quantitative audit of its behaviour (Chapters 3 and 8).',
       'A reproducible experimental harness of seeded simulations, sensitivity analyses and a token audit, plus a protocol for live repeated-run trials (Chapters 5 to 10).']),
('H2', '1.5 Scope and Organisation'),
('P', 'The analysis is based on the source code as it stood on 25 October 2026: 129 files and approximately 14,400 lines of TypeScript in the principal modules. All experiments were executed offline. No production database records were accessed and no live LLM calls were made, so no user data was read and no API quota was consumed. Results that depend on assumed input distributions are explicitly labelled as simulated.'),
('P', 'Chapter 2 reviews the literature. Chapters 3 and 4 describe the system architecture and the tokenization work. Chapter 5 sets out the experimental design, Chapter 6 the data pipeline and Chapter 7 the repeated-testing and validity analysis. Chapter 8 presents the results, Chapter 9 the visualisation layer and Chapter 10 the reproducibility evidence. Chapter 11 concludes.'),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
('H1', '2. Literature Review'),
('P', 'The review is organised around six themes. Together they reveal five research gaps, four of which DhanVeda addresses by design; the fifth, validation against market outcomes, remains open.'),
('H2', '2.1 Language Models for Financial Analysis'),
('P', 'Two strategies dominate the literature. The first trains a domain-specific model. BloombergGPT, a 50-billion-parameter model, was trained on 363 billion tokens of proprietary financial text plus 345 billion general-purpose tokens and outperformed general models on financial benchmarks [3]; the training budget, however, is far beyond the reach of a retail platform. The second strategy prompts a general model. Kim, Muhn and Nikolaev reported that GPT-4, given anonymised statements, predicted the direction of earnings changes more accurately than human analysts [4]. The authors later withdrew the paper after identifying data inconsistencies during replication. That withdrawal is itself instructive: claims about LLM performance in finance require independent replication before they are relied upon.'),
('H2', '2.2 Retrieval and Fabricated Figures'),
('P', 'FinanceBench evaluated 16 model configurations on questions about listed companies and found that GPT-4-Turbo combined with a retrieval system answered incorrectly or declined to answer in 81% of cases [5]. The implication for system design is that obtaining the correct figure is harder than reasoning about it. DhanVeda\'s collaborative mode responds directly to this finding by separating retrieval from scoring.'),
('H2', '2.3 Non-Determinism at Temperature Zero'),
('P', 'Atil et al. ran five models configured for deterministic output ten times each on eight tasks and observed accuracy differences of up to 15% between runs, with best-to-worst gaps of up to 70% [6]. For a product that reports a single numeric score, a single run should therefore be regarded as one sample from a distribution rather than as a definitive measurement. Chapter 7 quantifies this effect for the DV Score.'),
('H2', '2.4 Cost-Aware Routing and Model Cascades'),
('P', 'FrugalGPT demonstrated that querying models in order of increasing cost can match the quality of the strongest model at up to 98% lower expense [7], and RouteLLM trained routers on human preference data to more than halve cost without measurable quality loss [8]. Both approaches escalate on a learned estimate of answer quality. DhanVeda instead escalates on failure: rate-limit responses, empty output or unparseable JSON. This needs no training data, but it accepts any well-formed answer, including a wrong one.'),
('H2', '2.5 Prompt Compression and Tokenization'),
('P', 'LLMLingua uses a small language model to remove low-information tokens and reports compression ratios of up to 20 times with limited degradation [9]. Singh and Strouse showed that the way digits are grouped into tokens materially affects arithmetic accuracy; enforcing right-to-left grouping raised GPT-4 addition accuracy from 84.4% to 98.9% [10]. Because a financial rubric consists largely of numbers and thresholds, both its cost and its interpretation are sensitive to tokenization, as Chapter 4 demonstrates.'),
('H2', '2.6 Divergence of ESG Ratings'),
('P', 'Berg, Koelbel and Rigobon compared six leading ESG rating agencies and found pairwise correlations between 0.38 and 0.71. Differences in measurement accounted for 56% of the divergence, differences in scope for 38% and differences in weighting for 6% [11]. Coverage of Indian mid- and small-capitalisation companies by global agencies is limited, which makes SEBI\'s mandatory BRSR and pledge disclosures the most consistent structured signals available locally.'),
('H2', '2.7 Research Gaps'),
